(unfiltered this time — paste the whole thing, or at least anything mentioning gpio1 or gpio4, so I can see PDn# / GPIO1_IO00 and reset / GPIO4_IO21 actual line states)

Also worth trying a clean power-cycle of the module rather than just module reload, since firmware init failures are often sensitive to reset pulse width/timing that a modprobe -r/modprobe cycle won’t redo if the GPIO itself isn’t toggled as part of unload:

bash
modprobe -r moal mlan
sleep 2
modprobe mlan
sleep 1
modprobe moal fw_name=nxp/sd_w61x_v1.bin.se
dmesg | tail -30

If it fails identically again, the next thing to check is whether this new board’s PDn#/reset lines are actually connected the same way as the working one — worst case, a strapping or assembly difference on this unit. Paste back the -B10 -A5 context and the GPIO dump first.

